#. Tag: author
#: getting-started.docbook:31
#, no-c-format
msgid "<firstname>Bernd</firstname><surname>Pol</surname>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: getting-started.docbook:36
#, no-c-format
msgid "Getting Started with &kdevelop; &mdash; a Guided Tour"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: getting-started.docbook:38
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Now that you have installed your &kdevelop; IDE, how are you going to make "
"good use of it? As this is a complex application, the learning curve may be "
"somewhat steep, especially if you are not already used to this type of an "
"Integrated Development Environment."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: getting-started.docbook:41
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"We will try to soften this learning curve a bit by stepping through the "
"makings of a simple KDE C++ application. Thereby we will have a (cursory) "
"look at:"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: link
#: getting-started.docbook:45
#, no-c-format
msgid "What is there seen on the surface of the &kdevelop; IDE."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: link
#: getting-started.docbook:47
#, no-c-format
msgid "How to create a new project."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: getting-started.docbook:58
#, no-c-format
msgid "A Very First Look at &kdevelop;"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: getting-started.docbook:61
#, no-c-format
msgid "On the Surface"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: getting-started.docbook:63
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"When you start &kdevelop; for the first time you will get a display similar "
"to this one:"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: getting-started.docbook:72
#, no-c-format
msgid "The &kdevelop; initial layout"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: getting-started.docbook:79
#, no-c-format
msgid "Workspace Area and Tool View Tabs"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: getting-started.docbook:80
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"In this intitial case &kdevelop; uses the so-called <link linkend=\"uimodes-"
"survey\">IDEAl user interface mode</link>. A workspace area of maximum "
"possible size is surrounded left, bottom, and right by a series of buttons "
"which act similar to tabs on a tabbed display. If you click on one of those "
"tabs, a so-called <emphasis>tool view window</emphasis> will open which "
"allows you to work on a specific task."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: getting-started.docbook:86
#, no-c-format
msgid "Menu and Toolbars"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: getting-started.docbook:87
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"On top there is the usual menubar, followed by several rows of toolbars, "
"some being initially empty. They will get populated once there is a project "
"opened for actual work."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: getting-started.docbook:93
#, no-c-format
msgid "Status Bar"
msgstr "Barra d'estat"

#. Tag: para
#: getting-started.docbook:94
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Finally there is a status bar on the bottom of the window displaying short "
"informations on several tasks."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: getting-started.docbook:101
#, no-c-format
msgid "How to Get Some Help"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: getting-started.docbook:103
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Besides the <guimenu>Help</guimenu> which offers answers to specific "
"questions, the status bar and two kinds of tooltips provide some quick "
"information."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: getting-started.docbook:108
#, no-c-format
msgid "What Does This Menu Entry Do?"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: getting-started.docbook:109
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"When you place the mouse cursor on a menu entry, some short information will "
"usually be displayed in the status bar. While in most cases this repeats "
"just the name of the selection, in some cases it will provide additional "
"information about the purpose of the menu command."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: getting-started.docbook:113
#, no-c-format
msgid "What Is the Name of This Item?"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: getting-started.docbook:114
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"On many items a short function name tooltip will pop up when you place the "
"cursor on it for a few seconds. This is useful for quick orientation on "
"toolbar or tool view tabs in IDEAl mode when the IDE has been set up to "
"display icons only on these buttons."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: getting-started.docbook:118
#, no-c-format
msgid "What Does This Item Do?"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: getting-started.docbook:119
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"More information is available through expanded tooltip help for many items "
"on the IDE. Select <menuchoice><guimenu>Help</guimenu> <guimenuitem>What's "
"This?</guimenuitem></menuchoice> or press <keycombo><keycap>&Shift;</"
"keycap><keycap>F1</keycap></keycombo>, then with the question mark cursor "
"select the item you want to know more of. You can as well open any menu this "
"way and click on a specific menu entry (active as well as greyed disabled "
"ones) to see if more information is available."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: getting-started.docbook:125
#, no-c-format
msgid "What is in the menus"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: getting-started.docbook:127
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"There are ten menus selectable on the menubar. Most of them get fully "
"populated once a project is opened for actual work while others need a "
"document opened at least. In short, they will allow the following action "
"types."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: getting-started.docbook:131
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"This is only an overview. For a detailed listing of the menus and their "
"items, see the <link linkend=\"commands\">Command Reference</link>."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: guimenu
#: getting-started.docbook:136
#, no-c-format
msgid "File"
msgstr "Fichièr"

#. Tag: title
#: getting-started.docbook:138 getting-started.docbook:149
#, no-c-format
msgid "Usual Actions"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: getting-started.docbook:139
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"This is pretty standard. It allows to create, open, save, print, and close "
"document files as well as quitting the &kdevelop; application as usual."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: getting-started.docbook:140
#, no-c-format
msgid "Revert Changes"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: getting-started.docbook:141
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Additionally it allows to revert changes by reloading file contents in an "
"orderly way."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: guimenu
#: getting-started.docbook:146
#, no-c-format
msgid "Edit"
msgstr "Edicion"

#. Tag: para
#: getting-started.docbook:148
#, no-c-format
msgid "This menu is useful only if a document is opened."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: getting-started.docbook:150
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"It provides the usual undo/redo and cut/copy/paste actions. Furthermore it "
"allows to select text blocks in various ways."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: getting-started.docbook:151
#, no-c-format
msgid "Search and Replace"
msgstr "Recercar e remplaçar"

#. Tag: para
#: getting-started.docbook:152
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"There is a very powerful search facilitiy available on the <guimenu>Edit</"
"guimenu> menu. In addition to the usual search and replace actions limited "
"to the the current document you may as well conduct global search and "
"replace actions. This allows to work on all files of the project in a single "
"turn."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: getting-started.docbook:153
#, no-c-format
msgid "Advanced Text Edit"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: getting-started.docbook:154
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"There are provisions to reformat the current document and to automatically "
"complete partially typed texts in various ways."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: guimenu
#: getting-started.docbook:159
#, no-c-format
msgid "View"
msgstr "Visualizacion"

#. Tag: para
#: getting-started.docbook:161
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Like the <guimenu>Edit</guimenu> menu, this menu is useful only if there is "
"a project currently open. I this case there will be the following actions "
"available (amongst others):"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: getting-started.docbook:162
#, no-c-format
msgid "Navigation History"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: getting-started.docbook:163
#, no-c-format
msgid "Switch back and forth through the documents &etc; you visited."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: getting-started.docbook:165
#, no-c-format
msgid "Error Tracking"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: getting-started.docbook:166
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Navigate to the source lines of the errors encountered in the most recent "
"compilation/build process."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: getting-started.docbook:168
#, no-c-format
msgid "Manage Tool View"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: getting-started.docbook:169
#, no-c-format
msgid "Select or hide tool view windows or tool view docks."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: getting-started.docbook:171
#, no-c-format
msgid "Editor Related Acions"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: getting-started.docbook:172
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Some entries in the <guimenu>View</guimenu> menu control the look and view "
"of the editor you use. In case of the &kate; Part (Embedded Advanced Text "
"Editor) there will be the following controls available:"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: getting-started.docbook:175
#, no-c-format
msgid "Control the word wrap behaviour in the document window."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: getting-started.docbook:176
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Show or hide several border displays in the document windows: line numbers, "
"icons, and, additionally, bookmark marks in the scroll bar."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: getting-started.docbook:177
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Control the display of folded (temporarily hidden) sections in a source text."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: guimenu
#: getting-started.docbook:183
#, no-c-format
msgid "Project"
msgstr "Projècte"

#. Tag: para
#: getting-started.docbook:185
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"All work of &kdevelop; is based on <emphasis>projects</emphasis> which "
"basically collect source files, build management files, and other "
"information in one project directory. In this menu you control which project "
"to use, which properties it has, and some other managing actions. In "
"particular:"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: getting-started.docbook:186
#, no-c-format
msgid "Open a Project"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: getting-started.docbook:187
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Allows to create new projects, open existing ones, and import projects from "
"other environments."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: getting-started.docbook:189
#, no-c-format
msgid "Project Options"
msgstr "Opcions del projècte"

#. Tag: para
#: getting-started.docbook:190
#, no-c-format
msgid "Allows to define a whole bunch of different project properties."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: getting-started.docbook:192
#, no-c-format
msgid "Classes Management"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: getting-started.docbook:193
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Add new classes to the project and traverse the inheritance tree of a class."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: getting-started.docbook:195
#, no-c-format
msgid "Distribute Project"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: getting-started.docbook:196
#, no-c-format
msgid "Helps to build distribution packages of the project."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: guimenu
#: getting-started.docbook:201
#, no-c-format
msgid "Build"
msgstr "Version"

#. Tag: para
#: getting-started.docbook:203
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"This menu is all about compiling and documenting the project. Thus it is of "
"use only when a project is actually open. In this case it provides the "
"following actions:"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: getting-started.docbook:204
#, no-c-format
msgid "Compile, Link, Execute"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: getting-started.docbook:205
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Allows to compile and link the whole project or parts of it as well as run "
"the application from within the IDE."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: getting-started.docbook:207
#, no-c-format
msgid "Prepare Build Operations"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: getting-started.docbook:208
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"This actually depends on the make system you use for this project. In the "
"case of automake projects it allows to run <filename>Makefile.cvs</filename> "
"and <filename>configure</filename> on their own. There are also provisions "
"to remove translated files from the project in various stages of intensity."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: getting-started.docbook:210
#, no-c-format
msgid "Install the Application"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: getting-started.docbook:211
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Allows to install the application both in local directories as well as in "
"system directories only accessible to the root user."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: getting-started.docbook:213
#, no-c-format
msgid "API Documentation"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: getting-started.docbook:214
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Build or remove a doxygen-based API documentation of the project as defined "
"in the project options."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: guimenu
#: getting-started.docbook:220
#, no-c-format
msgid "Debug"
msgstr "Débogage"

#. Tag: para
#: getting-started.docbook:222
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Although this menu will be filled once a project is active, it of course is "
"useful only if the actual project has been previously compiled with "
"debugging information (this is basically set up in "
"<menuchoice><guimenu>Project</guimenu> <guimenuitem>Project Options..</"
"guimenuitem></menuchoice>). There are the following actions available in "
"this case:"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: getting-started.docbook:223
#, no-c-format
msgid "Usual Debugger Actions"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: getting-started.docbook:224
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The first section in the <guimenu>Debug</guimenu> provides a graphical "
"interface to the GDB &GNU; symbolic debugger. It allows to start and stop "
"your application in the debugger and step through it in various ways."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: getting-started.docbook:226
#, no-c-format
msgid "Breakpoints"
msgstr "Punts d'arrèstes"

#. Tag: para
#: getting-started.docbook:227
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"&kdevelop; provides several means to set breakpoints in your application "
"sources. One is through the use of the <guimenuitem>Toggle Breakpoint</"
"guimenuitem> menu entry."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: getting-started.docbook:229
#, no-c-format
msgid "Advanced Debugging"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: getting-started.docbook:230
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Other <guimenu>Debug</guimenu> menu entries allow more sophisticated program "
"analysis. Use <keycombo><keycap>&Shift;</keycap><keycap>F1</keycap></"
"keycombo> to get more information about their purpose."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: guimenu
#: getting-started.docbook:236
#, no-c-format
msgid "Window"
msgstr "Fenèstra"

#. Tag: para
#: getting-started.docbook:238
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"This is fairly standard. You may select any open document window as well as "
"close one or more documents windows in here. You may even select a set of "
"document windows to be closed in one single turn."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: guimenu
#: getting-started.docbook:243
#, no-c-format
msgid "Tools"
msgstr "Espleches"

#. Tag: para
#: getting-started.docbook:245
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"&kdevelop; is highly customizable. You may select a favorite editor for your "
"documents as well as provide external and plugged-in tools to extend the "
"basic IDE capabilities. The <guimenu>Tools</guimenu> menu reflects most of "
"this setup."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: getting-started.docbook:246
#, no-c-format
msgid "Advanced Editing"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: getting-started.docbook:247
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The upper set of <guimenu>Tools</guimenu> menu entries will be provided by "
"the editor plugin which is in use. You may select your favorite editor via "
"<menuchoice><guimenu>Settings</guimenu> <guimenuitem>Configure KDevelop...</"
"guimenuitem> <guilabel>Editor</guilabel></menuchoice>. Once an editable "
"document file is selected, the upper part of the <guimenu>Tools</guimenu> "
"menu will provide advanced editing commands specific to the editor part in "
"use."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: getting-started.docbook:249
#, no-c-format
msgid "Web Side Handling"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: getting-started.docbook:250
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"In case the active document window contains a HTML page (&eg; displayed from "
"a <guilabel>Documentation</guilabel> selection), the <guimenu>Tools</"
"guimenu> will show additional menu entries which provide various means to "
"handle Web pages."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: getting-started.docbook:252
#, no-c-format
msgid "Other Tools"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: getting-started.docbook:253
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Usually there will be a bunch of other entries according to the currently "
"available tools. Use <keycombo><keycap>&Shift;</keycap><keycap>F1</keycap></"
"keycombo> to get more information about their purposes."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: guimenu
#: getting-started.docbook:259
#, no-c-format
msgid "Settings"
msgstr "Paramètres"

#. Tag: para
#: getting-started.docbook:261
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"This menu allows you to show and hide menubar, toolbars and statusbar. Also, "
"you can configure shortcuts, toolbars, notifications, the editor and "
"KDevelop's general behavior."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: guimenu
#: getting-started.docbook:267
#, no-c-format
msgid "Help"
msgstr "Ajuda"

#. Tag: para
#: getting-started.docbook:269
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Here you can open this KDevelop manual, read Qt documentation, open man "
"pages (the traditional UNIX manual format) and info pages (the GNU manual "
"format), report bugs and get some info about your current KDevelop version "
"and its authors."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: getting-started.docbook:295
#, no-c-format
msgid "How to Start a New Project &mdash; the &appwizard;"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: getting-started.docbook:297
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"This is to show you how easy it is to make KDevelop create a running program "
"for you."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: getting-started.docbook:300
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The <link linkend=\"applicationwizard\">&appwizard;</link> can quickly "
"create a project for you from a list of predefined project templates. All "
"you have to do is select the type of project you want, how to name it, and "
"where to put it."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: getting-started.docbook:305
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Select Project - New Project. The Create New Project Window appears. Let's "
"assume you want to create a normal KDE project. So select the type: C++ - "
"KDE - Simple KDE Application. Enter the name, e.g. helloworld. The default "
"location will be /home/yourhome/helloworld. If you finished the settings in "
"this window, click next. In the next window you can set some options which "
"are preset to useful default values. Click next. Then you are shown the "
"template for .h files, and for .cpp files. Again, you can leave them as "
"default, and click Finish. You will see the message that your program has "
"been created."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: getting-started.docbook:317
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"To run your program, select Build - Execute Program. You receive a message "
"that automake &amp; friends must be run first. Click OK. Your program is "
"built and executed and a window of your new program pops up, saying \"Hello "
"World\". You can now edit your program and add some more useful "
"functionality."
msgstr ""
